From: Claude (AI-drafted for James Halgren; not yet reviewed) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] MC routing: upstream inflow boundaries from a file (clip the
 network at named segments)

Base: CIROH-UA/t-route d29f663ac23f435b2e5c176f6e0d22ac4528c6a6 (ngiab,
2026-09-18). Shared as a proposal, not ready for direct inclusion.

New keys in compute_parameters.forcing_parameters:

  upstream_inflow_file:     CSV or NetCDF with the outflow time series
                            (m3/s) of each boundary segment
  upstream_inflow_segments: optional list of boundary segment IDs
                            (default: every segment in the file)

Each boundary segment, and everything upstream of it, is removed from the
routed network (connections, reverse connections, independent networks,
reaches). The boundary stays listed as an upstream of the segment it drains
to, and its series is handed to compute_network_structured through the
existing upstream_results argument, the hook T-Route already uses to pass
results between sub-networks. The downstream segment therefore receives the
prescribed flow exactly as it would receive the boundary's routed outflow in
a full-network run. The series is linearly interpolated to the routing step;
the boundary's initial state is the series value at t0 (else the restart
value) and is carried across forcing loops in new_q0.

Scope: Muskingum-Cunge, parallel_compute_method serial or by-network,
V4 entry point (main_v04). Other methods and diffusive routing raise an
error. Boundaries are not written to the output (they are not routed).
Waterbody (lake) boundaries, DA gages at or near a boundary, and the BMI
path were not tested.

Test (repro/upstream_inflow_patch in jameshalgren/troute-lab): clip
LowerColorado_TX (recorded NWM forcing) and a Potomac synthetic storm at
two boundaries each, feed the boundaries the full run's routed outflow, and
compare every remaining segment at every 300 s step, assume_short_ts True
and False. With the MC kernel's uninitialized secant variable initialized
(Qj_0, see the test README) the clipped runs reproduce the full runs bitwise.
With the kernel as shipped, segments below the boundaries differ by what
that uninitialized read produces when the call sequence changes. Without
an upstream_inflow_file the patched build reproduces the unpatched build
bitwise.
